course: dictionary
lesson: 7137
title: Old words, old coins
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A pennyworth is an old word for the amount you could buy with one penny. In older British stories, children might buy a pennyworth of sweets, milk or bread. Say it as PEN-ee-wurth or PEN-uth.
explain: Context helps you here. The word can stand for an amount, as in «a pennyworth of sweets». It can also be part of an idiom, a set phrase that means something different from its single words.
explain: Today, people often use the idiom «Can I put in my two pennyworth?» to ask if they can give their opinion. In the US, people say «two cents' worth» instead.
fact: A pennyworth is an old-fashioned word, used mostly in the UK.
fact: «A pennyworth of sweets» means as many sweets as one penny would buy long ago.
fact: «Put in your two pennyworth» means to give your opinion.
fact: «Two cents' worth» is the US version of the same idiom.
fact: A tureen is a big, deep dish for serving soup, as in «Waiting in a hot tureen!»
exercise: «A pennyworth of sweets» means what? | A large box of sweets | *As many sweets as one penny would buy | A shop that sells only sweets | A penny with a sweet picture on it
exercise: Dad says, «Can I put in my two pennyworth?» What does he mean? | *To give his opinion | To count the coins in his pocket | To pay for the shopping
exercise: In «Beautiful Soup, so rich and green, Waiting in a hot tureen!», what is a tureen? | *A big, deep dish for serving soup | A green vegetable | A type of hot bread | A small spoon
test: «She bought a pennyworth of milk for the cat.» What does pennyworth mean here? | A large bottle | A cat's bowl | A jug of water | *The amount one penny would buy
test: In the US, what do people say instead of «two pennyworth»? | Two dollars' worth | Two nickels' worth | *Two cents' worth | Two pounds' worth
exam: Which sentence uses pennyworth to mean the amount a penny buys? | He ran a pennyworth down the road. | *She spent her penny on a pennyworth of sweets. | The tall pennyworth reached the ceiling. | We had a pennyworth for lunch.
